  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" covers the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It has about 45 fields, including `response` (string, with a `.keyword` subfield), `bytes`, `clientip`, `extension`, `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `url` and `timestamp`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created \"S4-c1af844d Web logs\" (ID `417388e9-9293-4a2c-84ab-b582a246c37e`) with exactly two panels. One is a line chart, \"Requests over time\", counting requests by `timestamp`. The other is a bar chart, \"Requests by response code\", counting requests by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created \"S4-c1af844d 5xx errors\" (ID `f4a38e82-c25b-46c4-adfa-76d42680f0c7`) as an Elasticsearch query rule. It was created already enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n   - **Query:** it counts every request whose `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\".\n   - **Schedule:** it runs every 1 minute and fires when the count in the last 5 minutes is greater than 10.\n   - **Actions:** it has none, so it won't notify anyone.\n4. **Panel edit:** the response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". Reading the rule back showed it enabled, and its last run finished with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
